1
00:00:00,695 --> 00:00:02,357
You know that moment when the evening

2
00:00:02,382 --> 00:00:05,342
changes... but your to-do list doesn't?

3
00:00:05,502 --> 00:00:07,717
That's the idea behind Watt If.

4
00:00:07,742 --> 00:00:10,197
Three flexible chores, a few boundaries,

5
00:00:10,222 --> 00:00:12,597
and a plan you can actually look at before

6
00:00:12,622 --> 00:00:15,142
saying yes. This is an Alexa Plus

7
00:00:15,422 --> 00:00:17,262
simulation. Let's walk through it.

8
00:00:19,436 --> 00:00:21,800
First, dryer and dishwasher by eight.

9
00:00:21,825 --> 00:00:23,400
Bike charged by ten.

10
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:24,905
I check the times...

11
00:00:25,025 --> 00:00:26,665
approve... and download.

12
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,995
Now, dryer at nine.

13
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:30,915
Quiet hours at eight.

14
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:32,405
That doesn't fit.

15
00:00:32,430 --> 00:00:34,660
Good. It refuses the request.

16
00:00:39,107 --> 00:00:40,202
Let's make room.

17
00:00:40,227 --> 00:00:42,002
Dryer finished by midnight;

18
00:00:42,027 --> 00:00:43,322
quiet after ten.

19
00:00:43,347 --> 00:00:45,082
It keeps the nine o'clock start.

20
00:00:45,107 --> 00:00:47,042
I review... approve...

21
00:00:47,067 --> 00:00:48,427
and download again.

22
00:00:50,495 --> 00:00:52,510
Here's what that last click gives us:

23
00:00:52,535 --> 00:00:54,430
a real calendar file.

24
00:00:54,455 --> 00:00:56,630
The dryer goes from nine to ten.

25
00:00:56,655 --> 00:00:58,375
The dishwasher and bike have their own

26
00:00:58,455 --> 00:01:00,615
slots. These are fictional household

27
00:01:00,695 --> 00:01:03,030
details, but the download is real.

28
00:01:03,055 --> 00:01:04,950
And there's an important boundary here.

29
00:01:04,975 --> 00:01:07,870
Approving a timetable only creates advisory

30
00:01:07,895 --> 00:01:09,990
calendar events. It doesn't turn anything

31
00:01:10,015 --> 00:01:12,615
on. You still decide what happens in your

32
00:01:12,735 --> 00:01:13,295
home.

33
00:01:14,928 --> 00:01:17,170
The interesting part is what happens between

34
00:01:17,195 --> 00:01:18,850
asking and approving.

35
00:01:18,875 --> 00:01:20,770
Strands runs the language model.

36
00:01:20,795 --> 00:01:22,730
The planning tool checks the changes against

37
00:01:22,755 --> 00:01:24,770
the request and the household rules.

38
00:01:24,795 --> 00:01:26,570
If the constraints clash, there's

39
00:01:26,595 --> 00:01:28,290
no fresh plan to approve.

40
00:01:28,315 --> 00:01:30,690
A correction creates a new revision,

41
00:01:30,715 --> 00:01:33,395
so yesterday's yes can't quietly become

42
00:01:33,435 --> 00:01:34,930
permission for something different.

43
00:01:34,955 --> 00:01:35,610
The evidence

44
00:01:35,635 --> 00:01:39,075
includes the requests, both approvals, and the files that

45
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:40,442
actually downloaded.

46
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:45,137
This recording used Qwen on a temporary GPU,

47
00:01:45,162 --> 00:01:47,270
through a private SSH connection.

48
00:01:47,295 --> 00:01:49,430
The app can also use local Ollama,

49
00:01:49,455 --> 00:01:51,910
or its manual controls without a model.

50
00:01:51,935 --> 00:01:54,550
Fifty-eight regression tests pass, alongside

51
00:01:54,575 --> 00:01:56,070
a separate solver check.

52
00:01:56,095 --> 00:01:57,590
Those are bounded checks,

53
00:01:57,615 --> 00:02:00,230
not a promise that every sentence will work.

54
00:02:00,255 --> 00:02:01,910
No live Alexa connection.

55
00:02:01,935 --> 00:02:03,430
No appliance control.

56
00:02:03,455 --> 00:02:05,430
No claimed energy savings.

57
00:02:05,455 --> 00:02:06,430
Just a little more

58
00:02:06,455 --> 00:02:09,335
room to see your choices... before you make them.
